AVONET trait data
-----------------

Bundled objects: avonet300 and avonet_full.

These objects contain subsets and transformations of AVONET. The AVONET
article and data are released under a Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY)
licence. Users must retain attribution to the original authors.

Source and citation:

Tobias JA et al. (2022). AVONET: morphological, ecological and geographical
data for all birds. Ecology Letters 25:581-597.
doi:10.1111/ele.13898

Data archive: https://figshare.com/s/b990722d72a26b5bfead

BirdTree phylogenies
--------------------

Bundled objects: tree300, trees300, tree_full, and tree_delhey.

These objects are pruned or sampled derivatives of the BirdTree Hackett
backbone and posterior trees. BirdTree requests citation of the source study,
but no explicit redistribution licence for these tree files has been located
in the package source or on the cited BirdTree material. A citation request is
not, by itself, permission to redistribute.

Source and citation:

Jetz W, Thomas GH, Joy JB, Hartmann K, Mooers AO (2012). The global diversity
of birds in space and time. Nature 491:444-448.
doi:10.1038/nature11631

Website: https://birdtree.org/

Release status: redistribution permission for the BirdTree-derived objects
must be documented before CRAN submission. If defensible permission cannot be
obtained, these objects must be removed from the release tarball or replaced
with clearly licensed synthetic/example phylogenies.

Delhey plumage and environmental data
-------------------------------------

Bundled object: delhey5809.

This object was derived from the data used by Delhey et al. (2019). The local
provenance record identifies the article and source CSV, but no explicit data
redistribution licence has been located. Accordingly, this notice does not
assert a licence for redistribution; permission or an explicit licence must be
verified before CRAN submission, or the object must be removed from the
release tarball.

Source and citation:

Delhey K, Dale J, Valcu M, Kempenaers B (2019). Reconciling ecogeographical
rules: rainfall and temperature predict global colour variation in the
largest bird radiation. Ecology Letters 22:726-736.
doi:10.1111/ele.13233

